+# Agent Note: Owned product presentation
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-10-15-owned-product-presentation.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Products need different presentation while the same Harness source retains its native defaults. Replacing feature dictionaries changes their owner and cannot reliably restore earlier copy when products unload in a different order. Runtime brand slots also arrive after the initial hero can render.
+
+## Decision
+
+The locale registry keeps native dictionary ownership and accepts separate partial copy contributions. Contributions are ordered per namespace, locale and key; each disposer removes only its own entries. Dictionary revisions refresh existing translation seats without changing language preference or Session data.
+
+Explicit product builds record public locale overrides and an initial hero mark URL. These inputs provide product presentation before runtime contributors activate. A runtime product plugin owns its additional contributions through Cordis effects. Unloading it reveals earlier runtime contributions and then the build or native defaults.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Patch native dictionaries and remove the native hero mark.** This changes unconfigured Harness behavior and makes product copy depend on feature source positions.
+
+**Mutate the registered dictionary or register a second dictionary owner.** Mutation bypasses revision notifications and reliable restoration; a second owner violates the single-owner dictionary API.
+
+**Use runtime slots alone.** Slots remain the runtime composition mechanism but do not supply presentation before their contributors activate.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Product-specific presentation is explicit build data or an owned runtime contribution. Products retain responsibility for their copy and assets. Locale fallback order, native dictionary ownership, durable preference, and Session content remain unchanged.
+
+The build record binds the public product inputs to its client artifacts. Registry disposal tests cover overlapping owners, late publication and fiber unload; hero tests cover native defaults and the explicit first-render fallback.
